2026 Oct 21 03:52:48 daemon.warning awplus chronyd[3784]: System clock wrong by 14.836050 seconds
2026 Oct 21 03:53:03 daemon.warning awplus chronyd[3784]: System clock was stepped by 14.836050 seconds
```
🔥 **That number is a cross-check of the whole group's instrumentation.** In T33399, with the
DUT acting as a *server*, `tools/ntpc.py` measured its clock from the box as **-14.835 s**
(min -14.835217, mean -14.834867). Here the DUT's own `chronyd`, measuring itself against the
box, says **14.836050 s**. Two completely independent instruments, opposite directions, and they
agree to about **1 millisecond**. The 16 s console estimate at the top of this log was right to
the second and useless beyond it; these two are the measurement.

Clock after: `show clock` 03:54:04 at console timestamp 03:54:05 -- aligned with the box.

**STEP 2 PASS.**

### STEP 3 -- The exchange and the control, both counted on the wire

`tools/ntpwire.py` on `t33400-s1-sync.pcap`:
```
03:52:59.170712 v4 10.38.22.34:42599 -> 10.38.22.33:123  client v4 LI=ok st=0 refid=----  xmit=04:39:39.534108
03:52:59.170985 v4 10.38.22.33:123   -> 10.38.22.34:42599 server v4 LI=ok st=5 refid=10.32.16.2 xmit=03:52:59.170929
03:53:01.196557 v4 10.38.22.34:36619 -> 10.38.22.33:123  client ... xmit=21:02:48.938483
03:53:03.201933 v4 10.38.22.34:37753 -> 10.38.22.33:123  client ... xmit=10:31:57.093908
03:53:05.214598 v4 10.38.22.34:54407 -> 10.38.22.33:123  client ... xmit=19:03:59.702545
03:54:10.147907 v4 10.38.22.34:50059 -> 10.38.22.33:123  client ... xmit=16:54:51.655083
03:55:14.834618 v4 10.38.22.34:55646 -> 10.38.22.33:123  client ... xmit=10:15:47.715640
03:56:19.438462 v4 10.38.22.34:44203 -> 10.38.22.33:123  client ... xmit=18:31:21.863853
  (each with its server reply)

  29 frames in the capture, 14 NTP packets on port 123
  by mode:   client(3)=7  server(4)=7
  10.38.22.34 -> 10.38.22.33   7
  10.38.22.33 -> 10.38.22.34   7
  pairing:   requests=7 replies=7  paired=7 unpaired=0 unanswered=0
```

- **7 polls, 7 replies, 7 paired, 0 unanswered** to the reachable server, and the count matches
  `show ntp counters`' `Client Valid Received 7` **exactly**. The device's counter and the wire
  agree.
- **ZERO NTP packets to 10.38.22.40.** The control did not merely fail to synchronise -- the
  DUT never got as far as sending it an NTP packet. The capture filter was
  `udp port 123 or arp host 10.38.22.40`, and of the 29 frames only 14 were NTP, so the
  remaining 15 are the DUT's unanswered ARP requests for 10.38.22.40: it tried, at layer 2, and
  nothing was there. That is the honest shape of this control and it is why the ARP clause was
  in the filter.
- **Poll cadence:** four polls 2 s apart at startup, then 64 s steady -- a burst-then-settle
  pattern, matching `Update interval 2.0 s` rising to `64.6 s`.

Two findings fell out of these eight lines, both of which would mislead a later reader:

🔥 **The DUT randomises its client source port on every poll** (42599, 36619, 37753, 54407,
50059, 55646, 44203). Any capture filter, ACL or management-filter rule written for
"source port 123" will miss **every** packet this client sends.

🔥 **The DUT randomises the TRANSMIT TIMESTAMP in its client requests** -- `04:39:39`,
`21:02:48`, `10:31:57`, `19:03:59`, `16:54:51`, `10:15:47`, `18:31:21`, none of them the real
time, all different. This is the NTP client-cookie privacy behaviour, and the exchange still
works because the server echoes the random value in its origin field (`paired=7` proves it).
**Anyone reading the client's transmit timestamp as "the DUT's clock" would conclude the DUT's
clock is wildly, randomly wrong.** It is not.

**STEP 3 PASS.**

### STEP 4 -- 🔴 A manual `clock set` while the client is running breaks it, and it does NOT recover by itself

A deliberate, known error injected into a working, synchronised client:

```
awplus#show clock              -> Wed, 21 Oct 2026 03:58:42 +0000     (synchronised)
awplus#clock set 03:00:00 21 October 2026
awplus#systemd-journald[138]: Time jumped backwards, rotating.
awplus#show clock              -> Wed, 21 Oct 2026 03:00:01 +0000     (-58 min 42 s)
```

90 seconds later:
```
awplus#show ntp status
Reference ID    : 00000000 ()
Stratum         : 0
Ref time (UTC)  : Thu Jan 01 00:00:00 1970
Leap status     : Not synchronised
awplus#show ntp associations
  10.38.22.33     10.32.16.2       5 u   44   64   377    0.538 3523229.   0.002
  10.38.22.40     -                0 u    -   64     0        -        -       -
```

Read that carefully, because it is the point of the step: **`reach 377`** -- every one of the
last eight polls succeeded -- and the association reports the offset (3 523 229 ms = 58.7 min),
and yet the `*` marker is gone, the stratum is 0, the reference ID is `00000000`, the reference
time is the UNIX epoch and the leap status is `Not synchronised`. **Packets are flowing
perfectly and the clock is not being corrected.** That is the §5 read-back trap inverted: here
the *traffic* is healthy and the *state* is broken, and only reading both tells you so.

The wire for this window (`t33400-s2-step.pcap`, 150 s):
```
  by mode: client(3)=2  server(4)=2 ; pairing: requests=2 replies=2 paired=2 unanswered=0
```
-- polling continued and was answered throughout, confirming it.

The DUT's own log, both jumps:
```
2026 Oct 21 03:00:00 user.notice awplus IMI[965]: clock set: previous time 03:58:43 21 Oct 2026, new time 03:00:00 21 Oct 2026
2026 Oct 21 03:00:12 daemon.warning awplus chronyd[3784]: Backward time jump detected!
2026 Oct 21 04:00:57 user.notice awplus IMI[965]: clock set: previous time 03:02:15 21 Oct 2026, new time 04:00:57 21 Oct 2026
2026 Oct 21 04:01:02 daemon.warning awplus chronyd[3784]: Forward time jump detected!
```
`chronyd` **detected** both jumps and logged them. It did not act on either.

The clock was then brought back by hand to within about 2 s of correct
(`clock set 04:00:57 21 October 2026`), and **75 s later it had still not been corrected**:
```
Reference ID : 00000000 ()  Stratum : 0  Leap status : Not synchronised
  10.38.22.33  10.32.16.2  5 u  36  64  377  0.499  2122.966  0.002
```
`Last offset` was frozen at `+0.000023680` -- byte for byte the value from before the injection,
i.e. the figure a reader might quote as "the current offset" is **stale** and does not update
while the daemon is unsynchronised.

**Honest grading of this limb** (and the sentinel's point, taken): the measurement is
*"it had not recovered within the windows allowed"*, not *"it can never recover"*. The windows
were **90 s** at an offset of 3523 s and **75 s** at an offset of 2.12 s. Whether `chronyd`
would eventually have slewed 2.12 s -- at a maximum slew rate of 500 ppm that is about
**70 minutes** -- is **NOT MEASURED**; nothing here distinguishes "refuses" from "slewing far
too slowly to see". What IS measured is that the device sat `Not synchronised` with a healthy,
fully reachable server for both windows.

**STEP 4 PASS** as a measurement, and it is Finding 1.

### STEP 5 -- Restarting the client recovers it in seconds, which pins the mechanism

The recovery path, and the strongest evidence in the step, because it changes one thing:

```
awplus(config)#no ntp server 10.38.22.33          (04:03:02)
awplus(config)#no ntp server 10.38.22.40
   -> show running-config | include ntp   : nothing
   -> show ntp status / associations / counters : all three COMPLETELY EMPTY again
awplus(config)#ntp server 10.38.22.33             (04:05:57)
```

```
tools/tb ckcon ... '@poll 300 Leap\sstatus.*Normal show ntp status'
@poll MATCHED after 1.2 s (1 reads)

Reference ID    : 0A261621 (10.38.22.33)
Stratum         : 6
System time     : 0.000000002 seconds fast of NTP time
Last offset     : -0.000002382 seconds
Leap status     : Normal
* 10.38.22.33   10.32.16.2   5 u  12  64  17  0.564  -0.000  0.003
```
**Synchronised within ~24 s of the re-add** (04:05:57 -> matched at 04:06:21), from a clock that
was 2.12 s out.

The log gives the mechanism, and the proof that it is a restart:
```
2026 Oct 21 04:06:07 daemon.warning awplus chronyd[4647]: System clock wrong by 2.122975 seconds
2026 Oct 21 04:06:09 daemon.warning awplus chronyd[4647]: System clock was stepped by 2.122975 seconds
```
**`chronyd`'s PID changed from 3784 to 4647** -- re-adding `ntp server` restarts the daemon --
and the restarted daemon stepped the clock in **2 seconds**. The stepped magnitude,
`2.122975 s`, matches the association's `offset 2122.966` ms read before the removal to within
**9 microseconds**.

So the mechanism is: **a running `chronyd` will not step a clock a `clock set` moved under it,
but a freshly started one will.** That is a product behaviour worth a developer's eye, and a
practical rule for anyone on this bench.

**STEP 5 PASS.**

### STEP 6 -- CONTROL: with the client removed, the DUT sends nothing at all

Measured in the gap between step 5's removal and re-add:
```
tools/tb capture tb277 ntp1021 eth3 110 t33400-s3-removed.pcap udp port 123

ntpwire: 0 frames in the capture, 0 NTP packets on port(s) 123
  NO NTP PACKETS. For a control leg that is the measurement, not an error.
```
**Zero frames in 110 s**, against a poll interval of 64 s before removal -- so the window
covered at least one poll that would have happened and did not. The client stops completely;
there is no residual polling. (`ntpwire` exit 2, the control exit code.)

**STEP 6 PASS.**

### STEP 7 -- Persistence across a cold boot, measured ON THE WIRE

```
awplus#write
Building configuration...
[OK]
```
The write added to startup-config exactly:
```
> !
> ntp server 10.38.22.33
```
plus its saved-on comment. Nothing else.

```
tools/tb ckreload tb277 ntp1021 u2 115200 240
17:07:42 >>> reload   (04:07:42 UTC)
reboot question: 'reboot system? (y/n):' -> y
17:08:42 login seen after 60 s
```
**60 s cold boot** -- a fourth measurement agreeing with group C's two and T33399's one.

**Nine seconds after the login prompt, with nothing typed into the DUT**, `t33400-s5-persist.pcap`:
```
04:08:51.165018 v4 10.38.22.34:48817 -> 10.38.22.33:123  client v4 LI=ok     st=0 refid=----
04:08:51.165147 v4 10.38.22.34:47275 -> 10.38.22.33:123  client v4 LI=ok     st=0 refid=----
04:08:51.165475 v4 10.38.22.33:123 -> 10.38.22.34:48817  server v4 LI=ok     st=5 refid=10.32.16.2
04:08:51.165522 v4 10.38.22.33:123 -> 10.38.22.34:47275  server v4 LI=UNSYNC st=0 refid=RATE
  pairing: requests=2 replies=2 paired=2 unpaired=0 unanswered=0
  !! 1 kiss-o'-death repl(ies), code(s): RATE
  !! 1 repl(ies) with LI=3 (server says it is NOT synchronised)
```

**The saved `ntp server 10.38.22.33` survived the cold boot and the client resumed polling on
its own.** That is the persistence limb, and it is proven by packets.

The same four packets also explain what happened next, and it is **not** a DUT defect:
`ntpwire` flagged a **kiss-o'-death, code `RATE`**, sent by **the testbox's own `ntpd`**, whose
stock configuration carries the `limited kod` restriction. The DUT's boot-time burst (two polls
in the same millisecond, from two source ports) tripped the *server's* rate limiter. The DUT's
own log agrees:
```
2026 Oct 21 04:08:50 daemon.warning awplus chronyd[2222]: Received KoD RATE from 10.38.22.33
```
and it then did the correct RFC thing: backed its poll interval off to 128 s and stopped using
the source (`refid RATE`, `reach 0`, `Stratum 0`, `Leap status Not synchronised`).

**STEP 7 PASS** (the configuration persisted and the client resumed, on the wire).

### STEP 8 -- Time to re-acquire synchronisation from cold, measured rather than assumed

At 180 s after boot the DUT was still `Not synchronised`, which on its own would have been an
ambiguous result (ordinary cold-start? too far off to step? a real failure to resume?). It was
therefore polled out to a longer window instead of graded early.

| time (UTC) | state |
| --- | --- |
| 04:07:42 | `reload` issued |
| 04:08:42 | login prompt -- **60 s** |
| 04:08:51 | first polls on the wire; one answered, one answered with **KoD RATE** |
| 04:09:01 - 04:12:01 | `@poll 180 ...` **TIMED OUT after 180.6 s (109 reads)** -- `Stratum 0`, `Reference ID 00000000`, `refid RATE`, `reach 0`, `Leap status Not synchronised` |
| 04:17:29 | backoff over, polling again: `10.38.22.33 ... reach 1 ... offset 593.552` ms, still `Not synchronised` |
| **04:19:34** | `chronyd[2222]: System clock was stepped by 0.592688 seconds` |
| **04:19:37** | `@poll ... MATCHED`: `Stratum 6`, `Reference ID 0A261621 (10.38.22.33)`, `Last offset -0.592688382 s`, `Leap status Normal`, `* 10.38.22.33 ... reach 7 ... offset 592.691` |

**Time from login prompt to synchronised: 10 min 55 s** -- of which roughly nine minutes were
the kiss-o'-death backoff caused by the *server's* rate limiter, not by the DUT. For comparison,
the same DUT against the same server with no KoD took **~75 s** from `ntp server` applied
(step 2) and **~24 s** on a daemon restart (step 5). It needed **four good samples**
(`Client Valid Received 4`, `reach 7`) at a 64 s poll before it would discipline the clock.

The stepped magnitude, **0.592688 s**, is also the third measurement of this DUT's drift across
a 60 s reboot: T33399 measured **0.57 s** of drift across its own reload from the box side. The
RTC keeps time across a power cycle and loses roughly half a second doing it.

**STEP 8 PASS** -- converged, with the window and the mechanism recorded. Nothing in this case
is graded on an association existing.

