## Teardown and verification

```
awplus(config)#no ntp server 10.38.22.33
awplus#show running-config | include ntp        -> nothing
awplus#write
```

- **running-config: 112 lines, byte-for-byte IDENTICAL to the pre-case baseline.**
- **startup-config differs from the campaign baseline by exactly its saved-on comment**
  (`... 04:21:13 ...` against `... 03:01:22 ...`). Nothing of this case survives there; the
  inherited underlay, vlan 60 and `lldp run` are all still present.
- 🔴 **The DUT's clock was NOT left where this case moved it.** It was deliberately skewed by
  -58 min 42 s in step 4, hand-corrected to within ~2 s, and then **corrected by NTP itself**:
  `chronyd` stepped it by 0.592688 s at 04:19:34 and the device was synchronised at 04:19:37.
  The client was removed only afterwards, so the DUT is free-running **from a correct value**:
  final read `show clock` = `Wed, 21 Oct 2026 04:21:17 +0000` against box `date -u` 04:21:20,
  i.e. within the console's own round-trip latency. The next case and the next session inherit
  a correct clock.
- `show boot` unchanged: `flash:/x960-tb277.rel`, `flash:/default.cfg`, Autoboot disabled.
- **swi_b and swi_a were never opened**, not even a bare CR.
- Nothing of this run is left on the box: every capture was a bounded
  `timeout <secs> tcpdump` and all had exited.

## Findings for `platforms/x960.md`

1. 🔴🔥 **A manual `clock set` while an NTP client is configured leaves the device
   unsynchronised, and a running `chronyd` does not recover it.** It logs
   `Backward/Forward time jump detected!`, drops to `Stratum 0` / `Reference ID 00000000` /
   `Ref time Thu Jan 01 00:00:00 1970` / `Leap status Not synchronised`, loses the `*`
   system-peer marker -- **while continuing to poll successfully (`reach 377`) and reporting
   the true offset in `show ntp associations`.** Measured over 90 s at a 3523 s offset and a
   further 75 s at a 2.12 s offset. Whether it would eventually slew is NOT MEASURED. Restarting
   the client (`no ntp server` / `ntp server`, which gives `chronyd` a new PID) steps the clock
   within **2 seconds** and re-synchronises within **24 s**.
2. 🔴 **`Last offset` in `show ntp status` is STALE while the device is unsynchronised** -- it
   held `+0.000023680 s` unchanged across two reads taken 75 s apart with a real offset of
   2.12 s. The live figure in that state is the association's `offset` column, not
   `Last offset`.
3. 🔥 **The NTP implementation is `chronyd`**, and it says so in the log
   (`daemon.warning awplus chronyd[<pid>]: ...`). Its PID changes when `ntp server` is
   re-applied, which is how you can tell a restart from a reconfiguration.
4. 🔥 **The client randomises its UDP source port on every poll** (seven consecutive polls used
   42599, 36619, 37753, 54407, 50059, 55646, 44203). A capture filter, a hardware ACL or a
   management filter written for "source port 123" misses every packet this client sends.
5. 🔥 **The client randomises the transmit timestamp in its requests** -- seven polls carried
   seven unrelated, wrong-looking times. The exchange works because the server echoes the random
   value in the origin field (`ntpwire` paired 7 of 7). **Reading that field as "the DUT's
   clock" gives a wildly wrong answer**, and it is the easiest mistake to make when grading an
   NTP client from a capture.
6. **A client's first four polls are 2 s apart, then it settles to 64 s**
   (`Update interval 2.0 seconds` -> `64.6 seconds`), and it needs **four good samples** before
   it will discipline the clock.
7. **An unreachable configured server costs nothing and is clearly marked**: `st 0`, `reach 0`,
   no `*`, no delay/offset/dispersion, and the poll interval backs off 64 -> 128 -> 256 s. The
   DUT never sends it an NTP packet at all -- it is still ARPing for it.
8. ⚠ **`show ntp counters` is client-only, and here it is exact**: `Client Valid Received 7`
   matched the wire's 7 paired replies precisely. (In T33399 the same three counters stayed at 0
   while the DUT served more than sixty requests as a *server*.) So the counters are
   trustworthy -- for one of the two roles.
9. **Time to re-acquire sync from cold: 10 min 55 s from the login prompt** on this bench, of
   which ~9 min was a kiss-o'-death backoff from the server. Without a KoD the same DUT syncs in
   **~75 s** from configuration and **~24 s** on a daemon restart. Nothing in the campaign
   recorded this before.
10. ⚠ **The DUT's boot-time poll burst can trip a stock `ntpd`'s `limited kod` restriction.**
    Two polls left the DUT in the same millisecond from two source ports; the second came back
    as a stratum-0 `RATE` kiss-o'-death and the DUT correctly backed off for about nine minutes.
    **This is a property of the test bench's server, not of the DUT** -- but on this bench it
    will happen again, so budget for it in any NTP client case here.
11. **`ntp server` entries are stored in reverse order of entry** in `show running-config`.
12. **`clock set` syntax is `clock set HH:MM:SS <1-31> <Month> <Year>`**, and it logs
    `IMI: clock set: previous time ..., new time ...` plus, on the console,
    `systemd-journald[138]: Time jumped backwards, rotating.`
13. **The RTC keeps time across a reboot and loses about half a second doing it** -- 0.57 s
    measured from the box in T33399 and **0.592688 s** measured by the DUT's own chronyd here.

## Tools used, with exact arguments

- `tools/tb cfg tb277 ntp1021 u2 115200 "configure terminal" "ntp server 10.38.22.33"
  "ntp server 10.38.22.40" "end" ...`
- `tools/tb ckcon tb277 ntp1021 u2 115200 "<reads>"` with `CKTO` 120-600, and the directives
  `@sleep <s>` and **`@poll <s> Leap\sstatus.*Normal show ntp status`** -- the poll directive is
  what turned "is it synced yet?" into a measured time-to-sync with an explicit window
  (`@poll MATCHED after 98.9 s (60 reads)`, `@poll TIMED OUT after 180.6 s (109 reads)`)
- `tools/tb qmark tb277 ntp1021 u2 115200 [--mode "configure terminal" --] "<prefix> "`
- `tools/tb ckreload tb277 ntp1021 u2 115200 240`
- `tools/tb capture tb277 ntp1021 eth3 <secs> <name>.pcap udp port 123 [or arp host 10.38.22.40]`
- **`tools/ntpwire.py`**: `tools/tb tool tb277 ntp1021 ntpwire /tmp/ntp1021/<f>.pcap [--quiet]`
- **`tools/ntpc.py`**: `tools/tb tool tb277 ntp1021 ntpc 127.0.0.1 --count 2` -- used here as the
  instrument control that proved the box's `ntpd` was synchronised at stratum 5 before the DUT
  was pointed at it
- `python3 tools/rc2cfg.py <ckcon stdout> tb277/x960/ntp-2026-10-21T1159/T33400/swi_d.cfg "<3 headers>"`

