- `tb277/x960/ntp-2026-10-21T1159/T33400/swi_d.cfg`
- `tb277/x960/ntp-2026-10-21T1159/T33400/work/run1.log` (this file)
- On the box, not committed: `t33400-s1-sync.pcap`, `t33400-s2-step.pcap`,
  `t33400-s3-removed.pcap`, `t33400-s4-resync.pcap`, `t33400-s5-persist.pcap`,
  `console-u2.log`, all in `/tmp/ntp1021/`.

**No `swi_b.cfg` and no `swi_a.cfg`**: both partner consoles are locked out.

VERDICT: PASS -- the x960-56YSQ is a working IPv4 NTP client as TPS row 261 claims: it polled the configured server and was answered 7 times out of 7 on the wire, reached stratum 6 from a stratum-5 source with a sub-millisecond offset, removed a 14.836 s clock error by stepping it (a figure that agrees to 1 ms with the same offset measured independently from the box in T33399), never sent a packet to the deliberately unreachable server configured beside it, fell completely silent when the configuration was removed, and resumed polling 9 s after a cold boot from startup-config and re-acquired synchronisation in a measured 10 min 55 s. Two product behaviours are called out rather than graded as failures: a manual `clock set` leaves a running chronyd unsynchronised until it is restarted (Finding 1), and the client's randomised source port and transmit timestamp will mislead anyone grading it from a capture (Findings 4 and 5).
